The code statements that lead to the bug are:
ns = alloc_ns(parent->base.hname, name);
if (!ns)
    return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
ns->level = parent->level + 1;
Reason:
The original code does not check the nesting depth (`parent->level`) before allocating a new namespace. This allows unlimited recursion in policy namespace creation, leading to exhaustion of system resources such as memory and kernel objects.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (parent->level > MAX_NS_DEPTH)
    return ERR_PTR(-ENOSPC);
Reason:
The added condition checks whether the parent namespace's depth already exceeds a maximum allowed depth (`MAX_NS_DEPTH`). If so, it returns an `-ENOSPC` error, preventing further nesting and thus limiting resource exhaustion.

